<p>Clearly, creating Mac apps that make the most of your voice continues to be a focus of developers — at least based on the offerings coming across my radar lately. Following <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/08/app-report-listen-read-checkmate/">last week’s profile</a> of two different takes on on-device transcription software, we’ve got yet another voice-powered app to take a look at, with this macOS download supporting dictation that you can then paste into other apps.</p>
<p>But other apps are finding different ways to make some noise, including a pair of simple-yet-satisfying utilities and an old favorite that’s learned some new tricks. If you’ve got an app that you’d like to bring to our attention, let us hear about it at <code>apps@sixcolors.com</code>, adding a brief description on what the app does and what devices it supports.</p>
<p>And now, on to the business at hand. Or at voice, as the case may be.</p>
<h2>Lesskeys</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/lesskeys1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Screenshot titled 'Transcribed' with a green checkmark, Copy button, and X icon." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>Some times, it’s better to dictate than to type, and <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/lesskeys-voice-to-text/id6783061182">Lesskeys</a> looks to make that process as simple as possible. With the app running in your menu bar, just hit a hotkey — it’s Option-Period by default — to start recording your dulcet tones. As you speak, your words are captured in a clipboard that you can copy and paste into any app once you stop recording by tapping the hotkey again. Alternatively, you can also hold down the hotkey to jot down quick thoughts with the recording stopping once you release the keys.</p>
<p>Lesskeys’ bag of tricks extends beyond just dictation. There’s a command for recording phone calls made through your Mac, and you can also import audio and video files to be transcribed. Additionally, the app boasts the ability to identify speakers when you record conversations. Power users will also appreciate how Lesskeys lets you set up different configurations for different apps, such as a different language, model or prompt when a particular app is in the foreground.</p>
<p>Lesskeys is free to download, but you’re limited to 5 minutes of dictation per day, along with five audio and video transcriptions each. That should be enough of a ceiling to test out the app and decided whether it’s worth the one-time $49.99 Pro license to remove any restrictions.</p>
<h2>PeekNote</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/peeknote-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Links for Self Promotion: App Report, LinkedIn Profile, Get your phil link." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>If you’re like me, you put the Mac’s clipboard through quite a workout, copy and pasting various text snippets, URLs and other captured material into different apps. And if you’re even more like me, you wind up using a lot of the same snippets and URLs over and over again. <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/peeknote/id6743180165?mt=12">PeekNote</a> is a handy macOS utility that keeps frequently used snippets close at hand.</p>
<p>Essentially, PeekNote is a floating window where you can stack multiple bits of text to paste into any window on your desktop. As I’m applying for a lot of jobs at the moment, I can use the PeekNote window to stash links to articles and other parts of my profile that I frequently use in cover letters and web forms.</p>
<p>Pay a one-time $9.99 fee, and you unlock the full app, which includes the ability to create 22 different tabs for organizing related text blocks. The unlocked version of PeekNote also lifts any limitations on the number of blocks.</p>
<p>If I had a suggestion for PeekNote, it would be to make it easier to shrink the app window so that it only expands when you need access to one of the text blocks you’ve got stored in there. But otherwise, it’s a fine utility when you want frequently used links and snippets to be right at your fingertips.</p>
<h2>Carrot Weather</h2>
<figure class="pull-right"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/carrot-merged-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="Two iPhone screens: left shows Locations with trips for Detroit, MI (Aug 15–17, 2026), favorites for Alameda, CA, and search bar; right shows Trip Details for Detroit, MI with dates and preview." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>If you’ve looked beyond the built-in Weather app on your iPhone, you doubtlessly have some familiarity with <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/carrot-weather-alerts-radar/id961390574">Carrot Weather</a> and its… <em>unique</em> take on delivering the forecast. The app, which works across a host of Apple devices including the Mac, Apple Watch and Vision Pro, tells you the weather with attitude, using a sliding snark scale that you can adjust to your liking. Additionally, as you can see from this <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2023/01/miss-dark-sky-carrot-weather-has-you-cloud-covered/">2023 Six Colors profile</a>, developer Grailr has built some other high-end capabilities and customization features into the app, the more intriguing of which are positioned behind a paywall.</p>
<p>Carrot Weather is worth revisiting these days thanks to an added feature that arrived over the summer — a trip planner capability for the iPhone version that lets you track the forecast for any upcoming getaways. I’ve got a trip to Detroit in my immediate future, and when I head over to the Locations section of Carrot Weather, I can see that I had better pack an umbrella for the thunderstorm that’s forecast on at least one day of my trip.</p>
<p>The 6.7 update to Carrot Weather also introduced new custom notifications that alert you when a data point hits a certain threshold — say, a temperature drop or a rise in UV index levels. Both the Trip Planner and custom notification features require a Premium Ultra subscription that costs $9.99/month.</p>
<h2>SmartTracker</h2>
<figure><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" src="https://i0.wp.com/sixcolors.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/smarttracker1-6c.png?ssl=1" alt="macOS app showing a price tracker." data-image-w="" data-image-h="" class=" jetpack-broken-image"><figcaption></figcaption></figure>
<p>With holiday shopping ramping up in the next few months, you’re going to want to stay on top of pricing to make sure that you’re paying the best price for anything on your wish list. Certainly, there are websites where you can do that — <a href="https://camelcamelcamel.com">CamelCamelCamel</a> is one of the better ones in my opinion — but an app like <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/smarttracker-price-tracker/id6754879283">SmartTracker</a> can pull off the same task while keeping your search data entirely on your Mac.</p>
<p>Enter a URL into the SmartTracker app, and it will do the watching for you; you’re even able to set a target price and get a notification should the object of your desire hit that magic number. Several other features such as home screen widgets, Siri shortcuts and collections for grouping items together will appeal to power shoppers.</p>
<p>SmartTracker runs on macOS, iOS and iPadOS, though I only looked at the Mac version of the app. You can track up to five items and manage three collections when you download the app, but paying $6.99/year (or a one-time $19.99 payment) gets rid of those limits.</p>
<h2>TuckBar</h2>
<p>Utilities don’t come any simpler than <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/tuckbar/id6779300947?mt=12">TuckBar</a>, which performs one task but does it well. You use the macOS utility to hide away the menu bar on displays of your choosing, only to bring it back the moment you move back toward the top edge of the screen.</p>
<p>That’s going to be welcome functionality if you use multiple displays and want to keep some of them truly uncluttered, while letting you keep the comfort of the menu bar on (presumably) your primary display. TuckBar mimics the appearance of your desktop wallpaper to obscure the menu bar, or if you prefer, the utility features a slider to turn down the menu bar’s brightness.</p>
<p>Normally, TuckBar costs $3 to download, though at time of writing, the app was free as part of a limited-time promotion.</p>
